Transitions: SDD

Purpose:
  Define default and conditional transitions between workflow stages.
  A transition is a directed edge from one stage to another, with
  conditions that must be satisfied before the transition is allowed.

  This file is the authoritative transition list — chains/chains.sdd ->
  DefaultFlow is a reference order, not the transition engine.


Owns:
  ./

Scope:
  Stage transitions
  Transition conditions
  Conditional transitions
  Default transitions
  Transition rules


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific flows (bax project/flows.sdd)
  Project-specific chains (bax chains/chains.sdd)
  Engineering skills (bax skills/)
  Task instances (bax tasks/)
  Project architecture (bax project/architecture.sdd)


DefaultFlow:
  AN > AR > DB > BE > API > FE > MD > QA > SC > DO > VR

  This is the reference order for a full-feature chain. Real chains
  skip/reorder/add stages per their own dependencies (bax chains/
  chains.sdd -> DefaultFlow, ProjectFlow, FlowResolution).


Transitions:

  Default transitions (always allowed when source stage is complete):

    AN     -> AR
    AR     -> BDD
    AR     -> DB
    BDD    -> ARCH
    ARCH   -> DB
    DB     -> BE
    BE     -> API
    API    -> FE
    FE     -> MD
    MD     -> QA
    BE     -> QA
    API    -> QA
    FE     -> QA
    MD     -> QA
    QA     -> SC
    SC     -> DO
    DO     -> VR
    VR     -> DONE

  Conditional transitions (allowed only when condition is met):

    AN     -> IMPACT    (when existing work is modified)
    IMPACT -> AR         (when architecture decisions are needed)
    IMPACT -> BE         (when bugfix has no architecture impact)
    AR     -> IMPACT    (when architecture decision reveals new impact)
    QA     -> BE         (when QA fails and failure is a BE defect)
    QA     -> API        (when QA fails and failure is an API defect)
    QA     -> FE         (when QA fails and failure is an FE defect)
    QA     -> MD         (when QA fails and failure is an MD defect)
    QA     -> DB         (when QA fails and failure is a DB defect)
    QA     -> ARCH       (when QA fails and failure is architectural)
    SC     -> BE         (when security finding requires BE fix)
    SC     -> API        (when security finding requires API fix)
    SC     -> DB         (when security finding requires DB fix)
    SC     -> ARCH       (when security finding requires architecture change)
    VR     -> BE         (when verification fails and failure is BE)
    VR     -> API        (when verification fails and failure is API)
    VR     -> FE         (when verification fails and failure is FE)
    VR     -> QA         (when verification fails and failure is QA)
    DO     -> QA         (when deployment reveals test gap)
    ANY    -> BLOCKED    (when dependency is unresolved or blocker exists)
    BLOCKED -> ANY       (when blocker is resolved, return to previous stage)


TransitionConditions:

  A transition is allowed only when ALL of the following are true:
    - Source stage has passed its gate (bax gates.sdd)
    - All dependencies of the source stage are satisfied
    - No blocker exists for the target stage
    - The target stage is in the task's ImpactMatrix with `+` or `?`

  AI MUST NOT force a transition that does not satisfy its conditions.


StageSkip:

  A stage MAY be skipped when:
    - It is not in the task's ImpactMatrix (marked `-`)
    - It is explicitly marked optional in the chain definition
    - Human has approved the skip

  AI MUST NOT silently skip a required stage (bax chains/chains.sdd ->
  [C7], tasks/tasks.sdd -> [T12]).


Rules:

  [TR1]  Every transition MUST have a declared source and target stage.

  [TR2]  Default transitions are the minimum required flow — conditional
         transitions expand the flow, they do not replace it.

  [TR3]  A task MUST NOT advance to a stage whose gate has not been
         defined (bax gates.sdd).

  [TR4]  A transition MUST NOT be taken if the source stage's output
         has not passed validation.

  [TR5]  Conditional transitions MUST declare their activation condition
         explicitly — AI MUST NOT invent conditions.

  [TR6]  A stage MAY have multiple outgoing transitions, but each MUST
         be distinguishable by its condition.

  [TR7]  A stage MUST have at least one outgoing transition.

  [TR8]  Transition order is determined by dependency resolution, not
         by the order listed in this file.

  [TR9]  A BLOCKED state does not create a new transition — it suspends
         the current stage until the blocker is resolved, then the
         original transition continues.

  [TR10] VR failure MUST trigger impact analysis before any backward
         transition is taken (bax chains/chains.sdd -> VerificationFailure).


State: +
  